---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { LINKS } from '@/lib/links';

import { LAST_UPDATED, formatLastUpdated } from '@/lib/page-dates.mjs';

const lastUpdated = formatLastUpdated(LAST_UPDATED.corpus);

// Rendered as the visible link text too. Derived rather than retyped so the URL
// cannot drift from `LINKS.corpus` — a link whose label disagrees with its href
// is exactly the failure links.ts exists to prevent.
const corpusLinkText = LINKS.corpus.replace(/^https:\/\//, '');

// The tiles below paint with `bg-brand-light-gray` / `text-brand-gray` rather
// than the `bg-card` / `text-muted-foreground` semantic utilities. Those resolve
// through `hsl(var(--card))`, but globals.css defines `--card` as a hex
// (`theme('colors.brand.light-gray')`), so the browser sees `hsl(#f6f6f6)`,
// discards it as invalid, and paints nothing. Fixing those tokens site-wide is
// its own change; these tiles just avoid depending on them.
//
// Every figure below is the count that actually SHIPS in the published
// parquet, taken from the export's own reconciliation ledger
// (`reconciliation.json`, alongside the data on the Hub) rather than from the
// source database. The two differ: the database holds 12,271 options and
// 5,286 endorsements, of which 4 and 3 respectively are quarantined by the
// export gates and never published. Quoting the database numbers would
// advertise rows a downloader cannot find.
const FIGURES = [
  { value: '230', label: 'concepts' },
  { value: '12,267', label: 'options' },
  { value: '5,283', label: 'endorsements' },
  { value: '76', label: 'contributors' },
  { value: '56', label: 'locales' },
];

// The four obligation-mapped source values. The wording of `meaning` and `owed`
// is held word-for-word with the dataset card's own table (CORE-1720): the site
// and the card describe one taxonomy, and a paraphrase here would read as a
// second, subtly different promise.
const SOURCES = [
  {
    value: 'legesher',
    meaning:
      'Produced by the Legesher project: our own packs, curated corrections, values we composed from existing pack material',
    owed: 'nothing beyond this licence',
  },
  {
    value: 'upstream',
    meaning: 'Lifted from another open-source project',
    owed: "that project's notice and credit, below",
  },
  {
    value: 'community',
    meaning: 'Offered by an individual through our native-review process',
    owed: 'credit on request, withdrawal on request',
  },
  {
    value: 'model',
    meaning: 'Generated by a language model',
    owed: 'disclosure of which model, below',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="The Language Corpus — Legesher"
  description="An open corpus of reserved-word translations for programming languages: every rendering anyone ever offered, every endorsement a person attached to it, and a provenance record saying what each one obliges you to do."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20 bg-white">
    <div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-4xl">
      <header class="mb-12">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-4">The language corpus</h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-muted-foreground mb-2">
          Every candidate translation of a programming-language concept that anyone ever offered &mdash; and every
          endorsement a native speaker attached to it. Openly licensed, and honest about where each word came from.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
          <span>Last updated: {lastUpdated}</span>
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-5 gap-4 mb-16">
        {FIGURES.map((figure) => (
          <div class="bg-brand-light-gray border border-brand-gray/30 p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-brand-cyan">{figure.value}</div>
            <div class="text-sm text-brand-gray">{figure.label}</div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <article class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:text-foreground prose-a:text-brand-cyan hover:prose-a:text-brand-coral prose-table:text-sm">
        <section aria-labelledby="what-it-is">
          <h2 id="what-it-is">What this corpus is</h2>
          <p>
            A concept-centric corpus of reserved-word translations for programming languages: every candidate
            rendering (&ldquo;option&rdquo;) of a programming-language concept in a human locale that anyone &mdash;
            Legesher, another open-source project, a community reviewer, or a model &mdash; ever offered, together
            with every endorsement a person attached to it.
          </p>
          <p>
            It exists because the question &ldquo;what is <code>if</code> in Telugu?&rdquo; does not have one answer.
            It has several, offered by different people at different times for different reasons, and the
            disagreements are as informative as the agreements. Most datasets in this space publish a single winning
            answer and discard the rest. This one keeps the evidence.
          </p>
          <p>
            Options are keyed by content-addressed, stable uids, so the corpus survives database rebuilds and
            re-exports byte-identically. It is append-only by design: a rendering that was later superseded stays,
            alongside the correction.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="canon-split">
          <h2 id="canon-split">What is deliberately not in it</h2>
          <p>
            This dataset is the <strong>corpus</strong>: the community evidence layer. The <strong>canon</strong>
            &mdash; which rendering Legesher actually ships per locale &mdash; is a maintainer decision that lives in
            our monorepo as reviewable text and is never published here. Neither is the private mapping from an
            opaque contributor id back to a person. The export tooling hard-fails rather than include either.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the corpus will not tell you what Legesher thinks the right answer is. That is on purpose: it is a
            record of what was offered and what was endorsed, not a verdict.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="provenance">
          <h2 id="provenance">How provenance works</h2>
          <p>
            Every row carries a <code>sources</code> list saying what kinds of source attested that rendering. The
            vocabulary is cut on one question &mdash; <em>what do we owe for this?</em> &mdash; so four values, four
            obligations:
          </p>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th><code>sources</code> value</th>
                <th>What it means</th>
                <th>What we owe</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {SOURCES.map((source) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><code>{source.value}</code></td>
                  <td>{source.meaning}</td>
                  <td>{source.owed}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p>
            That framing is the point. A taxonomy built around which script happened to insert a row goes stale the
            moment the pipeline changes; a taxonomy built around obligation stays true, because a future row's
            category is decided by asking what we owe for it.
          </p>
          <p>
            An option can carry more than one value: the same rendering is often proposed by a model and then
            independently attested by a reviewer. Counts per value are derivable from the data itself, and are
            deliberately not frozen into the dataset card &mdash; for the same reason endorsement counts are not.
          </p>
          <p>
            Row-level detail beyond these four values is deliberately not published: the finer-grained provenance
            field carries reviewer labels that identify people, and no part of this dataset is a route from an opaque
            contributor id back to a person. That is also why the upstream project names live in the dataset card
            rather than in a column &mdash; the obligation is machine-visible, the identity of the upstream project
            is human-readable.
          </p>
          <p>
            One thing worth saying plainly: <code>legesher</code> describes who produced a rendering, not how good it
            is. Like every other source kind, a Legesher-produced rendering has exactly the native-speaker standing
            its endorsements give it, and no more. Machine-generated renderings are a starting point, not an
            endorsement &mdash; which is why packs built from unattested corpus rows ship labelled experimental.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="licence">
          <h2 id="licence">Licence and attribution</h2>
          <p>
            The corpus is released under <strong>CC BY 4.0</strong>. You may share and adapt it, including
            commercially, with attribution.
          </p>
          <p>
            Part of it comes from other open-source projects that carry their own terms. Those terms are reproduced
            in full on the dataset card and travel with the data: CC BY 4.0 governs the <strong>compilation</strong>,
            and does not relicense the contributed parts.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <a href="https://github.com/legesher/legesher-translations" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
                >legesher-translations</a
              > &mdash; MIT. 149 contributors are credited in that repository&rsquo;s
              <a
                href="https://github.com/legesher/legesher-translations/blob/main/.all-contributorsrc"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer">.all-contributorsrc</a
              >, which stays the canonical roster and is deliberately not duplicated. This is a Legesher repository,
              so retaining the notice is a commitment we are keeping to those 149 people, not an obligation someone
              else is holding us to. We would owe them the credit either way.
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://github.com/UniversalPython/UniversalPython" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
                >UniversalPython</a
              > &mdash; Apache License 2.0, Urdu renderings. <strong>Changes were made:</strong> these renderings were
              adapted, not reproduced verbatim. They were normalised to our identifier conventions and re-keyed onto our
              concept slugs, and some were subsequently superseded by other options in the corpus. Do not read a rendering
              here as UniversalPython&rsquo;s own current form &mdash; consult the upstream project for that.
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://github.com/lenguaje-latino/latino" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Latino</a>
              &mdash; MIT, Spanish renderings.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Renderings marked <code>model</code> were produced by a language model:
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/CohereLabs/aya-101" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Aya 101</a>
            (Cohere For AI, Apache-2.0) and Claude (Anthropic, outputs owned by the API customer). Where a run recorded
            a specific model version it is retained; the earliest machine-generated rows predate version recording, so
            the card discloses the model family rather than promising a per-row version.
          </p>
          <p>
            Community-contributed translation data is never sold. It is not part of the datasets we license
            commercially &mdash; see <a href="/terms#licensing-model">our licensing model</a>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="withdrawal">
          <h2 id="withdrawal">If you contributed: credit and withdrawal</h2>
          <p>
            Contributors appear in the corpus as an opaque, salted identifier &mdash; never a name, handle, or email
            address. The mapping back to a person is held privately and is never included in any published copy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Credit is opt-in.</strong> You are anonymous by default, and your contribution counts the same
            either way. You may ask at any time to be credited by name, to change how you are credited, or to be
            removed entirely.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Withdrawal keeps the work and erases the person.</strong> We redact every column that identifies
            you and record that a withdrawal happened. The renderings you offered and the endorsements you gave
            remain as anonymous records &mdash; other contributors&rsquo; work depends on them &mdash; but nothing
            connects them to you. Because no published copy has ever carried an identity, a withdrawal never requires
            us to retract a release.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Two limits, stated plainly</strong>, because you would otherwise discover them at the worst
            moment. If you submitted through a public pull request or issue, that submission sits in a public
            repository under your own account, and we cannot unpublish something you published yourself. And where
            your details were recorded in our own repository, removing them is a change going forward; earlier
            revision history is cleared separately rather than on request. Ask and we will tell you where that
            stands.
          </p>
          <p>
            To be credited, change how you are credited, or withdraw, email
            <a href="mailto:privacy@legesher.com">privacy@legesher.com</a>. The full contributor notice ships in the
            project repository as <code>CONTRIBUTOR_PRIVACY.md</code>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="get-the-data">
          <h2 id="get-the-data">Get the data</h2>
          <p>
            The corpus is published on Hugging Face as five configs &mdash; <code>concepts</code>, <code>terms</code>,
            <code>options</code>, <code>endorsements</code>, <code>contributors</code> &mdash; with the full licence
            text, the upstream notices, and a reconciliation ledger alongside the data.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href={LINKS.corpus} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{corpusLinkText}</a>
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are a native speaker and something in there is wrong, we would rather know.
            <a href={LINKS.slack} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Find us in Slack</a> or open an issue on
            <a href={LINKS.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>.
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
